   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to the sale of new motor\nvehicle child restraint systems",
    "basePrintNo": "S300",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S300-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §399-iii, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S300\n\nSPONSOR: THOMAS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to the sale of new\nmotor vehicle child restraint systems\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nA bill to require retailers to post a warning and provide car seat alarm\ndevices to be sold alongside car seats in New York State.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the General Business Law by adding\n\nSection 399- uu to require that all retailers who sell car seats in New\nYork State also sell car seat alarm devices and provide a warning post-\ning.\n\nSection 2 provides that the statute shall take effect in ninety days.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew Yorkers were aghast by the recent tragedy of a father in the Bronx\nwho exited his motor vehicle while forgetting that his twin one-year old\nchildren were harnessed into car seats in the back seat, left to die\nalone in his overheated vehicle. The horror was profound, especially\nconsidering that memory is fallible and such accidents are preventable.\nAccording to www.kidsandcars.org, fifty-three children died from being\nleft in hot cars in 2018, and to date, in 2019, twenty-six children have\ndied in hot cars.\n\nThe technology exists to help prevent such avoidable deaths, and we\nshould take steps to ensure that such technology is available with the\nsale of all car seats sold in New York State. For example, Evenflo\nAdvanced SensorSafe Embrace DLX or Cybex Sirona M Sensorsafe are two car\nseats currently manufactured and sold with sensor technology. Other car\nseat warning devices are available to be added to existing car seats.\nThe proposed bill would also require retailers to post a warning for\nconsumers to educate and increase awareness. It is the goal of this bill\nthat utilizing such technology can help save lives.\n\nWe must take steps to protect these young children from unfortunate\nmistakes, and require that all car seats sold in the state of New York\nbe sold alongside with car seat alarm devices and a warning posting.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S.8604 (Thomas) - Referred to Rules Committee/A.8582 (Paulin)\nReferred to Consumer Affairs and Protection Committee.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone noted to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after 'it shall have\nbecome law.\n",
